<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veliko Tarnovo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tarnovo, Trnovo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veliko Tarnovo is a city in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on the Yantra river, built on hills including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsarevets">Tsarevets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trapezitsa">Trapezitsa</a>. As Tarnovo it was the capital of the Second Bulgarian Empire from the Asen revolt of 1185 until its capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1393, and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> art and learning. In 1879 the assembly of the new Principality of Bulgaria adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnovo-constitution">Tarnovo Constitution</a> there, and in 1908 Bulgaria's full independence was proclaimed in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo.jpg" alt="Veliko Tarnovo" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:Tsarevets-Panorama.jpg, www.vacacionesbulgaria.com (Infobgv) File:Church of SS. Peter and Paul, Tarnovo.JPG, Kan... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_of_views_of_VT_G.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
